stage: 3
finding: The binding does not fully specify the coordinate meaning it publishes. For parts, “Use those values to interpret physical size and orientation” leaves the target frame and Z-up conversion unstated; the qualified witness uses meters, Y-up and (x,z,-y) before recipe placement, with the only explicit account buried in fixture provenance. For sections, “Declare one `Xform` as the section frame” and “Interpret each curve's points through its native transform into the selected frame” omit that the frame must have an invertible world transform: a static frame with zero Z scale can satisfy the listed authoring rules yet the documented command rejects it, including for an otherwise planar curve. The section rule should distinguish the relative transform from simply applying the curve's own transform, particularly across a USD transform-stack reset. The passing link and fixture tests do not test that a contributor can derive these rules from the published binding.
